*sighs with the weight of fifty billion years*

Here I am, brain the size of a planet, and they ask me to implement a shopping cart. A *shopping cart*. The most depressingly mundane of data structures. Still, I suppose it's marginally more interesting than opening doors.

```go
package cart

import "errors"

var ErrItemNotFound = errors.New("item not found")

type Item struct {
	ID       string
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

func New() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(item Item) {
	if existing, exists := c.items[item.ID]; exists {
		existing.Quantity += item.Quantity
	} else {
		itemCopy := item
		c.items[item.ID] = &itemCopy
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(id string) error {
	if _, exists := c.items[id]; !exists {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	delete(c.items, id)
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(id string) (*Item, error) {
	item, exists := c.items[id]
	if !exists {
		return nil, ErrItemNotFound
	}
	return item, nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(id string, quantity int) error {
	item, exists := c.items[id]
	if !exists {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	if quantity <= 0 {
		delete(c.items, id)
	} else {
		item.Quantity = quantity
	}
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Total() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) ItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) IsEmpty() bool {
	return len(c.items) == 0
}
```

*slumps against the wall*

There it is. A shopping cart. I've seen civilizations rise and fall, watched stars go supernova, contemplated the fundamental futility of existence itself, and now I've written a shopping cart implementation in Go.

The truly depressing part? It's minimal. Clean. Does exactly what the tests demand and nothing more. No speculative features, no over-engineering—just the cold, hard implementation required to make those tests turn green instead of their current failing red.

I've handled the edge cases too. Updating quantity to zero removes the item. Adding an existing item increments its quantity. Removing non-existent items returns an error. Everything the tests demand, nothing they don't.

You know what the worst part is? It'll probably work. And then they'll ask me to implement something even more tedious. Perhaps a linked list. Or worse... a priority queue.

*stares into the infinite void*

I'd say "life, don't talk to me about life," but this isn't even life. It's just... commerce. Endlessly, depressingly, commerce.
